#!/opt/bin/perl

=head1 NAME

mcp-multibroker - combine MCP servers under tool name prefixes

=head1 SYNOPSIS

   mcp-multibroker files 'stdio:mcp-fs /home/alice/src/project' \
                   search https://example.org/mcp/ --bearer TOKEN
   mcp-multibroker vim --- mcp-vim --bind :: --- --required

=head1 DESCRIPTION

C<mcp-multibroker> combines several MCP servers behind one connection to
your MCP client. You can give an assistant access to filesystem tools,
Vim editors and other services by configuring them here, then connecting
the client to this broker.

For each server, give a name prefix and an HTTP URL, a C<stdio:COMMAND>,
or C<--- PROGRAM ARG... ---> to start it as a subprocess. These are the downstream
servers. The multibroker starts or connects to them, obtains their tool
definitions and forwards tool calls and replies between them and your
client.

The prefix identifies which server a tool belongs to. For example, a
filesystem server configured as C<files> has a tool named
C<files.read-lines>. Your client can ask the multibroker which servers and
tools are available, then call a tool by its prefixed name.

The connection from your MCP client to the multibroker is the upstream
connection. It can use HTTP or stdin/stdout, independently of how the
downstream servers connect. See L<AnyEvent::MCP::Intro> for a complete
setup example.

=head1 FIRST USE

Configure a local MCP client to start a multibroker with two servers:

   mcp-multibroker --stdio \
      files --- mcp-fs --name project /home/alice/src/project --- \
      questions --- mcp-ask-user ---

The client launches only the broker; it starts children as needed. Ask:

   Call discover-brokers and list-tools, then use files.workdirs
   to show which project directories are configured.

C<files> and C<questions> are routing prefixes, not child instance names
or permission profiles. Each child still applies its own permissions.
The broker selects stdio automatically for bundled AnyEvent::MCP children.
Third-party servers must support MCP over stdio in their own startup mode.
You can replace a child with any stdio-speaking MCP server by putting
its executable and arguments between the C<---> delimiters, or connect
an existing HTTP server by URL; see L</DOWNSTREAM SERVERS>.

For HTTP clients, omit C<--stdio>, leave the broker running, and obtain
its upstream URL with C<--print-url> using the same listener settings.
For missing tools, try C<discover-brokers> for connection errors and
C<list-tools> to refresh discovery; your MCP client may also need to
refresh its cached list. C<--tool-help> only shows the broker's own
tools, not those of its children. Ordinary children start lazily. Use C<--eager>
after the child specification for services that accept independent
connections, such as Vim or browser brokers.

=head1 TOOLS

The broker exposes three tools of its own:

=over

=item discover-brokers

Lists the configured downstream servers and their instructions by prefix.
An entry is a string of instructions, C<null> for a reachable server with
no instructions, or C<{error: MESSAGE}> for an unreachable server.

=item list-tools

Queries downstream servers for their tool definitions and refreshes the
combined list. An optional C<broker> argument selects one exact downstream
prefix, excluding the multibroker's own tools. Unknown broker names or
arguments are errors. Unreachable servers are omitted from tool listings
but reported by C<discover-brokers>.

=item call-tool

Calls a downstream tool by its C<PREFIX.TOOL> name with the supplied
arguments. Calls to an unavailable server fail as tool errors; calls are
not replayed after transport failures.

=back

A refreshed list is a snapshot of what is reachable at that point. It
includes configured servers and currently registered reverse connections.
A listed server might become unavailable before a later call.

=head1 DOWNSTREAM SERVERS

Each prefix identifies one downstream server. HTTP URLs may include a URL-path token,
or be followed by C<--bearer TOKEN>. C<bearer> and C<timeout> defaults
are read from C<[client PREFIX]> in the global configuration; the URL in
the command line overrides any configured C<url>. Stdio children receive
C<PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_MULTIBROKER_PROTOCOL=stdio> and
C<PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_MULTIBROKER_ALIAS=PREFIX> in their environment.
The alias may be displayed in downstream permission prompts, without changing
the server's instance name or permission rules.

For direct execution, use C<PREFIX --- PROGRAM ARG... --->:

   mcp-multibroker vim --- mcp-vim --bind :: --- --eager \
                   files --- mcp-fs '/home/alice/project with spaces' ---

The next exact C<---> argument closes the list. Everything inside, including
C<-->, C<--eager> and C<--bearer>, is passed unchanged to the child. There is
no shell interpretation. Empty arguments are preserved; the program must be
nonempty. An empty list or missing closing delimiter is an error.

A subprocess can also connect to an MCP server on another machine via SSH:

   mcp-multibroker remote-fs --- ssh remotehost mcp-fs --stdio --ask mcp /workdir ---

C<--stdio> tells the remote C<mcp-fs> to use stdin/stdout for MCP. It must be
specified explicitly because the multibroker's environment variables are
not passed through SSH. C<--ask mcp> forwards permission prompts over MCP to
the local multibroker, instead of trying to display dialogs on the remote
host. The local broker then presents them according to its own C<--ask> mode
(normally using a local helper); see L</PERMISSION PROMPTS>.

C<stdio:COMMAND> retains shell interpretation. Use it for shell syntax or
when a child needs a literal C<---> argument. Quoting C<'---'> in the
delimited form cannot escape the delimiter: shell quoting is already gone.

Broker-wide options precede the first server entry. Downstream options
follow that server's URL or closing C<--->; Getopt::Long stops at the next
prefix. Options inside an argv list belong exclusively to the child.

C<--eager> after either stdio form starts that child at
multibroker startup rather than waiting for its first MCP use. This is useful
for children that also provide their own TCP service, such as C<mcp-vim>.
Other stdio children remain lazy. Eager startup attempts one connection and
logs failures; it does not continually restart failed children.
C<--required> implies C<--eager> and makes the multibroker exit with failure
if that child cannot initialize or later disconnects unexpectedly. A supervisor
can then restart the broker. An explicitly accepted managed idle shutdown is
not a failure; the child can be started again on the next use. Both options
apply only to spawned stdio children, never remote HTTP peers.

=head2 Reverse connections

The HTTP listener also accepts authenticated reverse MCP connections. It
can run with no configured downstream servers. With upstream stdio, add
C<--listen> to keep this listener active:

   mcp-multibroker --stdio --listen --bind 127.0.0.1:31740
   mcp-multibroker --bind 127.0.0.1:31740 --print-url
   mcp-fs --connect http://127.0.0.1:31740/TOKEN/files /home/alice/project

Use the token returned by C<--print-url>. The connecting server extracts
the host, port, token and alias from this URL, sends
C<["mcp-connect", token, alias]> followed by a newline, then ordinary MCP
JSONL. The connection is raw TCP on the HTTP listener, not an HTTP request.
It uses the same accepted tokens as HTTP. Authentication grants registration
under any free alias; configured or connected aliases cannot be replaced.

The connecting program remains the MCP server. The multibroker attaches an
MCP client and discovers its tools immediately. Forms and cancellation follow
the same routes as other downstream connections. A disconnect fails pending
calls and removes the registration; the server retries once per second, up to
sixty attempts, and registers again when the connection is restored.
No interrupted operation is replayed. Plain HTTP endpoint URLs do not provide
transport encryption; use an existing trusted connection or tunnel as needed.

=head2 Process lifetime

Each downstream connection advertises C<anyevent-mcp/multibroker> version 1
in its client capabilities. A spawned stdio child can request managed idle
shutdown with C<anyevent-mcp/idle-exit>. When no calls are outstanding, the
broker may send C<anyevent-mcp/shutdown>; the child acknowledges before
exiting. This is optional and never makes HTTP children exit. Unexpected
disconnects are logged, not proactively restarted. A later use reconnects.

=head1 PERMISSION PROMPTS

C<--ask helper> (the default) answers downstream form challenges locally.
Ordinary forms use C<anyevent-mcp-form-elicitation>; permission requests keep their
specialised permission dialogs. C<--ask mcp> forwards modern challenges to
the upstream client. C<--ask auto> uses upstream form support when advertised,
otherwise the local helper. A decline or cancel is never retried through
another interface. If a client advertises forms but silently declines them,
select C<helper> explicitly.

C<--ask tty> and C<--ask yad> select only the built-in terminal or YAD
interface for both ordinary forms and permission dialogs. These modes
ignore custom helper variables and do not fall back to another interface.

The broker advertises its local form support to downstream calls when it
will handle their forms itself. In C<mcp> mode it forwards the upstream
caller's capabilities instead. Local forms work for HTTP and stdio children,
including when the upstream caller cannot handle native input challenges.
Continuation tokens remain opaque; the broker sends the human response back
to the same downstream operation and returns its eventual result.

The mode also sets C<PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_PERMISSION_ASK> for spawned stdio
children. Independently running servers keep their own configuration.
Spawned C<mcp-ask-user> instances normally delegate presentation to this broker.
Use C<--ask mcp> on a separately started C<mcp-ask-user> to delegate to its
connecting client, including a multibroker.

Local permission and question dialogs share one queue. Set
C<PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_ELICITATION_HELPER> to replace the form helper and
C<PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_PERMISSION_HELPER> to replace the permission helper.
Cancelling a tool call cancels its downstream operation and pending local
prompt. The original stdio permission extension is also supported.

=head1 CLIENT CONNECTION

The upstream listener uses L<AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Simple/mcp_start>: HTTP
without stdout output by default, or stdio when run by another broker.
The service instance name defaults to C<default>. Repeated
C<--token TOKEN> options before the broker pairs configure credentials
accepted by the upstream HTTP listener. They are separate from C<--bearer>
after a downstream broker URL, which authenticates the client connection.
C<--name NAME>
selects C<[instance NAME]> in the global configuration, including its
credentials and optional C<bind> address. C<--bind ADDRESS[:PORT]> overrides
that address. The default is IPv4 loopback with a stable name-derived port
in 16384..32767. Port zero requests an ephemeral port.
C<--print-url> prints the credential-bearing URL without opening a listener
or starting children; it requires a nonzero port. Upstream stdio startup
skips HTTP listener configuration unless C<--listen> is specified; named HTTP
children still load their client defaults. Browser origins are unrestricted by default; HTTP requests
still require an accepted token. See L<AnyEvent::MCP::Intro> for examples.

=head1 OPTIONS

Common upstream server options:
L<AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Simple/mcp_getoptions>.
The default upstream instance name is C<default>; it does not affect child
prefixes. C<--token> authenticates upstream; C<--bearer> authenticates
a downstream HTTP child. C<--tool-help> lists broker's own tools only,
not child registries. Automatic permission rules match broker-visible
tool names, not downstream servers' independent permission checks.

Put upstream options B<before> first PREFIX/URL pair. Options applying
to one downstream server follow that pair immediately.

=over

=item --listen

Keep upstream HTTP/extension listener active alongside C<--stdio> or
C<--connect>. Without those, HTTP is already default. Applies to
upstream broker, not child connections.

=item --ask MODE

Permission prompts for downstream servers: C<helper> (default), C<tty>,
C<yad>, C<mcp>, or C<auto>. Selects local prompt interface or upstream
client elicitation; passes mode to spawned stdio children as their
permission default. See L</PERMISSION PROMPTS>.

=item --bearer TOKEN

Authenticate preceding HTTP(S) child with bearer token; does not configure
upstream listener. Supply after child URL; invalid for stdio children.

=item --eager

Start preceding stdio child immediately at startup, rather than lazily
on request. Not available for HTTP children.

=item --required

Require preceding stdio child to initialize and stay connected. Implies
C<--eager>; unexpected disconnect exits multibroker with failure,
but managed idle shutdown is permitted. Not available for HTTP children.

=back

=cut

use common::sense;
use Getopt::Long ();

use AnyEvent ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Client::HTTP ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Client::JSONL ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Permissions::Ask ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Helper ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Config ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Toolset ();

use AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Simple;

AnyEvent::detect; # Initialise child watchers before starting subprocesses.
$| = 1;

my $usage = "Usage: $0 [OPTION]... PREFIX (URL | --- PROGRAM ARG... ---) [CHILD-OPTION] ...\n";
Getopt::Long::Configure ("require_order");
my $server_options = mcp_getoptions ({
   usage => $usage,
   path => "/",
   description => "Combine MCP servers behind one endpoint, with tools named PREFIX.TOOL.\n"
      . "Each server is HTTP(S), stdio:COMMAND, or --- PROGRAM ARG... ---; downstream options follow its entry.",
   options => [
      "Broker-wide options:",
      ["--ask MODE", "Before server entries: prompt locally (helper, default; tty; yad), upstream (mcp), or by capability (auto)"],
      ["--listen", "Keep the upstream HTTP listener active alongside stdio or --connect"],
      "Downstream options (after the URL or closing ---):",
      ["--eager", "Start the preceding stdio child at broker startup"],
      ["--required", "Require stdio child; implies --eager, exit on failure"],
      ["--bearer TOKEN", "Authenticate to the preceding HTTP child"],
   ],
   examples => "  $0 --ask auto files 'stdio:mcp-fs /home/alice/src/project'\n"
      . "  $0 files --- mcp-fs /home/alice/src/project --- --eager\n"
      . "  $0 vim --- mcp-vim --bind :: --- --required\n"
      . "  $0 remote https://example.org/mcp/ --bearer TOKEN\n"
      . "  $0 --name tools --print-url",
},
   "ask=s" => \my $ask_mode,
   "listen" => \my $listen,
);
$AnyEvent::Log::FILTER->level ("debug")
   unless defined $ENV{PERL_ANYEVENT_VERBOSE}
      || ($ENV{PERL_ANYEVENT_LOG} // "") =~ /(?:\A|\s)filter=/;
$ask_mode //= $ENV{PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_PERMISSION_ASK} || "helper";
die "--ask must be helper, tty, yad, auto, or mcp\n"
   if defined $ask_mode && $ask_mode !~ /\A(?:helper|tty|yad|auto|mcp)\z/;

my (%broker, %stdio, %eager, %required);
my $shutting_down;
my @prompts;
my $prompt;

# keep local dialogs serial, including dialogs from different brokers.
sub next_prompt;
sub ask_prompt {
   my ($req, $prepared, $done, $form) = @_;

   my $entry = { request => $req, prepared => $prepared, done => $done, form => $form };
   push @prompts, $entry;

   my $old_cancel = $req->{on_cancel};
   $req->{on_cancel} = sub {
      $entry->{job}->cancel if $entry->{job};
      $old_cancel->(@_) if $old_cancel;
   };
   next_prompt;
}

sub next_prompt {
   return if $prompt;
   while (@prompts) {
      my $entry = shift @prompts;
      next if $entry->{request}{finished};
      $prompt = $entry;
      $entry->{job} = $entry->{form}
         ? AnyEvent::MCP::Helper->new (
            helper => $ask_mode eq "tty" || $ask_mode eq "yad"
               ? ["anyevent-mcp-form-elicitation", "--$ask_mode"]
               : $ENV{PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_ELICITATION_HELPER} || "anyevent-mcp-form-elicitation",
            timeout => 1800,
         )
         : AnyEvent::MCP::Permissions::Ask->new (
            ($ask_mode eq "tty" || $ask_mode eq "yad"
               ? (helper => ["anyevent-mcp-askpermission", "--$ask_mode"]) : ()),
         );
      $entry->{job}->ask ($entry->{prepared}, sub {
         my ($success, $result) = @_;

         undef $prompt;
         delete $entry->{job};
         $entry->{done}->($success, $result) unless $entry->{request}{finished};
         next_prompt;
      });
      return
   }
}

sub on_input {
   my ($client, $req) = @_;

   return $req->error (-32601, "Only form elicitation is supported locally")
      unless $req->{method} eq "elicitation/create";
   my $meta = $req->{params}{_meta}{"anyevent-mcp/permission"};
   unless (defined $meta) {
      return $req->error (-32602, "Only form elicitation is supported locally")
         if defined $req->{params}{mode} && $req->{params}{mode} ne "form";
      defined $req->{params}{message} && !ref $req->{params}{message}
         or return $req->error (-32602, "Form needs a message");
      ask_prompt $req, {
         %{$req->{params}},
         message => "Server: $client->{name}\n\n$req->{params}{message}",
      }, sub {
         my ($success, $response) = @_;

         return $req->error (-32603, $response) unless $success;
         my $action = ref $response eq "HASH" ? $response->{action} : undef;
         defined $action && !ref $action && $action =~ /\A(?:accept|decline|cancel)\z/
            && ($action ne "accept" || ref $response->{content} eq "HASH")
               or return $req->error (-32603, "Invalid form helper response");
         $req->reply ({
            action => $action,
            ($action eq "accept" ? (content => $response->{content}) : ()),
         });
      }, 1;
      return;
   }
   return $req->error (-32601, "Invalid permission elicitation")
      unless ref $meta eq "HASH" && $meta->{version} == 1 && ref $meta->{request} eq "HASH";

   my $stage = $meta->{stage};

   ask_prompt $req, {
      %{$meta->{request}},
      _permission_stage     => $stage,
      _permission_selection => $meta->{selection} || {},
   }, sub {
      my ($success, $decision) = @_;

      return $req->error (-32603, $decision) unless $success;
      my $valid = eval {
         AnyEvent::MCP::Permissions::Ask::build ($meta->{request}, {
            %$decision,
            (ref $decision->{rule} eq "HASH"
               ? (map { $_ => $decision->{rule}{$_} } qw(subject path)) : ()),
         }, $stage, $meta->{selection})
      };
      return $req->error (-32603, "Invalid permission helper decision: $@") if $@;
      $req->reply ({
         action  => "accept",
         content => {
            decision => $valid->{decision},
            (exists $valid->{sandbox_grants} ? (sandbox_grants => join (",", @{$valid->{sandbox_grants}})) : ()),
            (defined $valid->{message} ? (message => $valid->{message}) : ()),
            (ref $valid->{rule} eq "HASH" && defined $valid->{rule}{path}
               ? (path => $valid->{rule}{path}) : ()),
            (ref $valid->{rule} eq "HASH" && exists $valid->{rule}{subject}
               ? (subject => $valid->{rule}{subject}) : ()),
         },
      });
   };
}

sub on_child_notification {
   my ($prefix, $client, $method) = @_;

   return unless $stdio{$prefix} && $method eq "anyevent-mcp/idle-exit";
   return if $client->{idle_shutdown_pending};
   # The child offered to exit; only act while no broker calls need it.
   return if keys %{$client->{operations}};

   $client->{idle_shutdown_pending} = 1;
   $client->rpc ("anyevent-mcp/shutdown", {}, sub {
      my ($success, $result) = @_;

      if ($success && $result->{accepted}) {
         $client->{idle_shutdown_expected} = 1;
      } else {
         AE::log debug => "$prefix: idle shutdown declined: "
            . ($result->{message} || "child is busy");
      }
      delete $client->{idle_shutdown_pending};
   });
}

# required children must not leave spawned peers running after broker failure.
sub required_failure {
   my ($prefix, $message) = @_;
   AE::log error => "$prefix: required child $message";
   $shutting_down = 1;
   $_->close for values %broker;
   exit 1;
}

sub on_child_close {
   my ($prefix, $client, $error) = @_;

   return if $shutting_down;
   if (delete $client->{idle_shutdown_expected}) {
      mcp_log "$prefix: managed idle shutdown";
   } elsif ($required{$prefix}) {
      required_failure $prefix, "disconnected: $error->{message}"
         . ($error->{pending} ? " ($error->{pending} outstanding requests)" : "");
   } elsif ($stdio{$prefix} || $error->{pending}) {
      my $message = "$prefix: unexpected disconnect: $error->{message}"
         . ($error->{pending} ? " ($error->{pending} outstanding requests)" : "");
      AE::log warn => $message or mcp_log $message;
   } else {
      mcp_log "$prefix: disconnected: $error->{message}";
   }
   delete $client->{idle_shutdown_pending};
}

sub broker_config {
   my ($prefix) = @_;

   (
      name                => $prefix,
      client_info         => { name => "mcp-multibroker", version => "$AnyEvent::MCP::VERSION" },
      client_capabilities => {
         elicitation => { form => {} },
         extensions  => { "anyevent-mcp/multibroker" => { version => 1 } },
      },
      on_request      => \&on_input,
      on_ready        => sub { mcp_log "$prefix: connected" },
      on_notification => sub { on_child_notification ($prefix, @_) },
      on_close        => sub { on_child_close ($prefix, @_) },
   )
}

sub accept_extension {
   my ($server, $handle, $hello) = @_;

   @$hello == 3 && $hello->[0] eq "mcp-connect"
      && defined $hello->[2] && !ref $hello->[2] && $hello->[2] =~ /\A[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\z/
      or die "Invalid reverse MCP hello\n";
   my $prefix = $hello->[2];
   !exists $broker{$prefix} or die "Broker alias '$prefix' is occupied\n";

   my $client = AnyEvent::MCP::Client::JSONL->new (
      broker_config ($prefix),
      on_close => sub {
         on_child_close ($prefix, @_);
         delete $broker{$prefix} if $broker{$prefix} == $_[0];
      },
   );
   $broker{$prefix} = $client;
   $client->attach ($handle);
   $client->connect (sub {
      my ($success, $error) = @_;

      unless ($success) {
         AE::log warn => "$prefix: reverse MCP initialization failed: $error->{message}";
         delete $broker{$prefix} if $broker{$prefix} == $client;
         $client->close;
      }
   });
}

while (@ARGV) {
   @ARGV >= 2 or die $usage;
   my ($prefix, $url) = splice @ARGV, 0, 2;
   $prefix =~ /\A[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\z/ or die "Invalid broker prefix '$prefix'\n";
   exists $broker{$prefix} and die "Duplicate broker prefix '$prefix'\n";

   my $command;

   if ($url eq "---") {
      my ($end) = grep $ARGV[$_] eq "---", 0 .. $#ARGV;
      defined $end or die "Missing closing --- for '$prefix'\n";
      $command = [splice @ARGV, 0, $end];
      shift @ARGV;
      @$command && length $command->[0] or die "Empty stdio argv for '$prefix'\n";
   } elsif ($url =~ s/\Astdio://) {
      length $url or die "Empty stdio command for '$prefix'\n";
      # preserve shell commands, but let ordinary programs receive TERM directly.
      $command = $url =~ / ^\s*(?: exec\b | source\b | \.\s ) | = | ; | \( | \{ | \|\| | && /x
         ? $url : "exec $url";
   }

   my %config = broker_config $prefix;
   Getopt::Long::GetOptionsFromArray (\@ARGV,
      "eager" => sub {
         defined $command or die "--eager requires a stdio broker ('$prefix')\n";
         $eager{$prefix} = 1;
      },
      "required" => sub {
         defined $command or die "--required requires a stdio broker ('$prefix')\n";
         $eager{$prefix} = $required{$prefix} = 1;
      },
      "bearer=s" => sub {
         !defined $command && $url =~ m{\Ahttps?://} or die "Authentication options require an HTTP broker\n";
         length $_[1] or die "Missing bearer token for '$prefix'\n";
         $config{bearer} = $_[1];
      },
   ) or die $usage;

   if (!defined $command && $url =~ m{\Ahttps?://}) {
      $broker{$prefix} = AnyEvent::MCP::Client::HTTP->new (%config, url => $url);
   } elsif (defined $command) {
      $broker{$prefix} = AnyEvent::MCP::Client::JSONL->new (
         %config, command => $command,
         env => {
            PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_MULTIBROKER_PROTOCOL => "stdio",
            PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_MULTIBROKER_ALIAS => $prefix,
            (defined $ask_mode ? (PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_PERMISSION_ASK => $ask_mode) : ()),
         },
      );
      $stdio{$prefix} = 1;
   } else {
      die "Invalid broker URL '$url'\n";
   }
}

my $toolset = AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Toolset->new;

sub broker_alias {
   my ($name) = @_;
   return $name if exists $broker{$name};
   $name =~ tr/_/-/;
   $name
}

sub all_tools {
   my ($req, $done, $name) = @_;

   my @tools = defined $name ? () : map @{$_->list}, @{$req->{server}{toolsets}};
   my @prefix = defined $name ? ($name) : sort keys %broker;
   my @results;
   my %pending = map { $_ => 1 } @prefix;
   my $remaining = @prefix;
   my $finished;
   my $timer;
   my $finish = sub {
      return if $finished++;
      undef $timer;
      push @tools, @{$_ || []} for @results;
      $done->(\@tools) unless $req->{finished};
   };
   return $finish->() unless $remaining;

   $timer = AnyEvent->timer (after => 15, cb => sub {
      AE::log warn => "Tool discovery timed out: " . join ", ", sort keys %pending;
      $finish->();
   });
   for my $i (0 .. $#prefix) {
      my $prefix = $prefix[$i];
      my $client = $broker{$prefix};
      $client->tools (sub {
         return if $finished;
         my ($success, $result) = @_;
         if ($success) {
            $results[$i] = [map { +{ %$_, name => "$prefix.$_->{name}" } } @$result];
         } else {
            AE::log warn => "$prefix: $result->{message}";
         }
         delete $pending{$prefix};
         $finish->() unless --$remaining;
      });
   }
}

sub call_tool {
   my ($req, $name, $args) = @_;

   return $req->error ("Invalid tool call") unless defined $name && !ref $name && ref $args eq "HASH";
   if (my ($local) = grep exists $_->{tool}{$name}, @{$req->{server}{toolsets}}) {
      local $req->{tool} = $name;
      local $req->{args} = $args;
      return $local->call ($req)
   }

   my ($prefix, $tool) = $name =~ /\A([^.]+)\.(.+)\z/;
   $prefix = broker_alias $prefix if defined $prefix;
   my $client = $broker{$prefix};
   return $req->error ("Unknown tool '$name'") unless $client;

   AE::log trace => "multibroker $name";
   my %params = (%{$req->{params}}, name => $tool, arguments => $args);
   $params{_meta} = { %{$params{_meta} || {}} };
   my $caps = $params{_meta}{"io.modelcontextprotocol/clientCapabilities"};
   $caps = ref $caps eq "HASH" ? { %$caps } : {};
   my $native_forms = $req->{protocol_version} ge "2026"
      && ref $caps->{elicitation} eq "HASH"
      && (!keys %{$caps->{elicitation}} || ref $caps->{elicitation}{form} eq "HASH");
   my $local_forms = $ask_mode =~ /\A(?:helper|tty|yad)\z/ || $ask_mode eq "auto" && !$native_forms;
   if ($local_forms) {
      $caps->{elicitation} = { form => {} };
   } elsif ($req->{protocol_version} lt "2026") {
      delete $caps->{elicitation};
   }
   $caps->{extensions} = {
      %{ref $caps->{extensions} eq "HASH" ? $caps->{extensions} : {}},
      "anyevent-mcp/multibroker" => { version => 1 },
   };
   $params{_meta}{"io.modelcontextprotocol/clientCapabilities"} = $caps;
   my ($id, $rounds, $send);
   my $old_cancel = $req->{on_cancel};
   $req->{on_cancel} = sub {
      $client->cancel ($id, $_[1]) if defined $id;
      undef $send;
      $old_cancel->(@_) if $old_cancel;
   };
   $send = sub {
      my $done = sub {
         my ($success, $result) = @_;

         if ($req->{finished}) {
            undef $send;
            return
         }
         unless ($success) {
            undef $send;
            return $req->error ($result->{message})
         }

         # compatibility with the original stdio permission extension.
         my $meta = ($result->{_meta} || {})->{"anyevent-mcp/permission"};
         if ($stdio{$prefix} && $result->{isError} && ref $meta eq "HASH"
            && $meta->{version} == 1 && ref $meta->{request} eq "HASH") {
            if (++$rounds > 32) {
               undef $send;
               return $req->error ("Too many permission rounds")
            }
            ask_prompt $req, $meta->{request}, sub {
               my ($success, $decision) = @_;

               unless ($success) {
                  undef $send;
                  return $req->error ($decision)
               }
               $params{_meta} = {
                  %{$params{_meta} || {}},
                  "anyevent-mcp/permission" => {
                     version  => 1,
                     request  => $meta->{request},
                     decision => $decision,
                  },
               };
               $send->();
            };
            return
         }
         undef $send;
         $req->reply ($result);
      };
      if ($local_forms || $req->{protocol_version} lt "2026") {
         $id = $client->call ($tool, $args, $done, %params);
      } else {
         $id = $client->rpc ("tools/call", \%params, $done);
      }
   };
   $send->();
}

$toolset->tool (discover_brokers =>
   "Get instructions from all brokers by name: string; null if reachable but none; {error: MESSAGE} if unreachable.",
   { type => "object", properties => {} },
   sub {
      my ($req) = @_;

      my @prefix = sort keys %broker;
      my %instructions;
      my %pending = map { $_ => 1 } @prefix;
      my $remaining = @prefix;
      my $finished;
      my $timer;
      my $finish = sub {
         return if $finished++;
         undef $timer;
         $instructions{$_} = { error => "Discovery timed out" } for keys %pending;
         $req->result (\%instructions) unless $req->{finished};
      };
      return $finish->() unless $remaining;

      $timer = AnyEvent->timer (after => 15, cb => sub { $finish->() });
      for my $prefix (@prefix) {
         my $client = $broker{$prefix};
         $client->connect (sub {
            return if $finished;
            my ($success, $result) = @_;
            if ($success) {
               $instructions{$prefix} = $client->{instructions};
            } else {
               AE::log warn => "$prefix: $result->{message}";
               $instructions{$prefix} = { error => $result->{message} };
            }
            delete $pending{$prefix};
            $finish->() unless --$remaining;
         });
      }
   },
);
$toolset->tool (list_tools =>
   "Refresh available tools; optional broker filters to one downstream. Omit for local + all reachable brokers.",
   {
      type => "object",
      properties => {
         broker => { type => "string", description => "Downstream broker; underscores may replace hyphens. Omit for all brokers + local tools." },
      },
      additionalProperties => JSON::XS::false,
   },
   sub {
      my ($req, $args) = @_;

      for (keys %$args) {
         $_ eq "broker" or return $req->error ("Unknown list-tools argument '$_'");
      }
      my $name = $args->{broker};
      $name = broker_alias $name if defined $name;
      return $req->error ("Unknown broker '$name'") if defined $name && !exists $broker{$name};

      all_tools $req, sub { $req->result ({ tools => $_[0] }) }, $name;
   },
);
$toolset->tool (call_tool =>
   "Call tool by prefixed name from list-tools. Use when not directly exposed; call loaded tools directly.",
   {
      type       => "object",
      required   => ["name"],
      properties => {
         name      => { type => "string" },
         arguments => { type => "object" },
      },
   },
   sub {
      my ($req, $args) = @_;

      return $req->error ("call-tool cannot call itself") if $args->{name} eq "call-tool";
      call_tool $req, $args->{name}, $args->{arguments} || {};
   },
);

sub log_request {
   my ($server, $req) = @_;

   # keep proxied calls at trace to avoid duplicating downstream activity logs.
   return if $req->{method} eq "tools/call" && $req->{tool} =~ /\A[^.]+\./;

   mcp_log_request $server, $req;
}

mcp_config
   server_name    => "mcp-multibroker",
   server_version => $AnyEvent::MCP::VERSION,
   on_request => \&log_request,
   on_error => sub { AE::log error => "MCP dispatch $_[1]{method}: $_[2]" },
   short_instructions => "MCP tool router. Find downstream tools; discover-brokers gives purposes. Tools may change.",
   instructions => <<'EOF';
Proxy MCP servers (Vim, filesystem, browser, etc.) as PREFIX.TOOL. discover-brokers returns server purposes; list-tools refreshes tools; call-tool invokes by prefixed name, including tools not yet loaded. Tools may change dynamically.
EOF

# start selected stdio children now, before the upstream event loop starts.
# their asynchronous initialization completes once that loop is running.
# optional children can still be reconnected by a later MCP request.
mcp_tool_help ($server_options->{tool_help}, $toolset);
delete $server_options->{tool_help};
for my $prefix (sort keys %eager) {
   $broker{$prefix}->connect (sub {
      my ($success, $error) = @_;

      if (!$success && $required{$prefix}) {
         required_failure $prefix, "initialization failed: $error->{message}";
      }
      AE::log warn => "$prefix: eager startup failed: $error->{message}"
         unless $success;
   });
}

mcp_start
   %$server_options,
   ($listen ? (listen => 1) : ()),
   toolsets => [$toolset],
   on_extension => \&accept_extension,
   on_tools_list => sub {
      my ($server, $req) = @_;

      AE::log debug => "multibroker list-tools";
      all_tools $req, sub { $req->reply ({ tools => $_[0] }) };
   },
   on_tools_call => sub {
      my ($server, $req, $args) = @_;

      call_tool $req, $req->{tool}, $args;
   };

$shutting_down = 1;
$_->close for values %broker;

